    "art": "flame",
    "excerpt": "A conversation game with no score and no pressure.",
    "body": "<p class=\"lead\">A preference menu can turn vague curiosity into an easier conversation. It works best when both people understand that the menu is about interests, not obligations, and that even a yes is not permission for future activity.</p><h2 id=\"try-this\">A practical idea</h2><p>Write ideas for nonsexual and romantic connection on separate cards: slow dancing, a shared bath, a massage, a new date spot, a long kiss, or a night of uninterrupted conversation. Sort each into yes, maybe, or no. Nobody has to explain a no.</p><h2 id=\"real-life\">What it might look like</h2><p>One person marks massage as maybe because they dislike scented oils. The couple learns that an unscented hand massage might be appealing while a full-body massage is not.</p><h2 id=\"boundaries\">Boundaries and wellbeing</h2><p>Do not use the list as a negotiation ledger. A maybe means more discussion, not persuasion. Check in again if circumstances change, and never interpret silence as agreement.</p><h2 id=\"conversation\">A question to take with you</h2><p>Which 'maybe' would be easiest to talk about without deciding whether to try it?</p><p>Neither person owes participation, disclosure, or physical intimacy. Preferences can change. Ask, listen, and make it easy to decline.</p><h2 id=\"deeper-context\">Build a menu worth discussing</h2><p>Use ordinary and romantic possibilities together: cooking a new meal, slow dancing, a long kiss, a shared bath, trying a new restaurant, or a screen-free evening. Sorting them alongside one another reduces the impression that every conversation must end with sex. Each person can create their own menu before comparing answers.</p><h2 id=\"practical-framework\">What a maybe really means</h2><p>Maybe can mean interested but unsure, curious only in theory, or interested under specific conditions. Ask which interpretation fits, but allow someone to say they do not know. A maybe never authorizes an activity and should not become a target for persuasion. When plans change, ask again rather than relying on an old list.</p><h2 id=\"real-world-decisions\">Turn overlap into an invitation</h2><p>Find one idea both people independently marked yes. Discuss timing, comfort, privacy, and what would make it easy to stop. If there is no overlap today, choose a different way to connect. The purpose is learning about each other, not reaching a quota of shared preferences.</p><h2 id=\"small-adjustments\">Small adjustments that matter</h2><p>Include everyday affection alongside romantic activities so the conversation does not become a disguised sexual checklist. For each maybe, ask what information would help: privacy, timing, a description of the plan, or more time. It is also fine to leave a maybe unresolved indefinitely.</p>",
    "practice": "Which 'maybe' would be easiest to talk about without deciding whether to try it?",
    "tool": "conversation",
